### Turn

A turn is the atomic unit of conversation - any interaction a participant intentionally communicates.

```typescript
interface Turn {
  id: TurnId;
  conversationId: ConversationId;
  participant: ParticipantId;
  timestamp: Timestamp;

  // Content - generic model
  contentType: string;           // Well-known or custom (x-prefixed)
  content: unknown;              // Shape determined by contentType

  // Threading
  threadId?: ThreadId;
  inReplyTo?: TurnId;

  // How this turn was created
  source: TurnSource;

  // Optional
  visibility?: TurnVisibility;
  status?: 'pending' | 'streaming' | 'complete' | 'failed';
  metadata?: Record<string, unknown>;
}

type TurnSource = 
  | { type: 'explicit'; method: 'mail/turn' }        // Direct mail/turn call
  | { type: 'intercepted'; messageId: MessageId };    // Auto-recorded from map/send + mail meta
```

### Content Types

#### Well-Known Types (Protocol-Defined)

| contentType | content shape | Purpose |
|-------------|---------------|---------|
| `text` | `{ text: string }` | Human-readable message |
| `data` | `{ [key: string]: unknown }` | Structured JSON payload |
| `event` | `{ event: string, data?: unknown }` | Lifecycle/system event |
| `reference` | `{ type: string, id: string, uri?: string }` | Pointer to another entity |

These are the only types the protocol defines. Clients that understand only these 4 types can still render any conversation meaningfully.

#### Custom Types (Application-Defined)

Custom types use `x-` prefix. These are defined by frameworks and implementations, not the protocol:

| contentType | Example content | Defined by |
|-------------|----------------|------------|
| `x-tool-call` | `{ tool: 'search', input: {...}, status: 'running' }` | Agent framework |
| `x-tool-result` | `{ output: {...}, durationMs: 450 }` | Agent framework |
| `x-thought` | `{ reasoning: '...', confidence: 0.8 }` | Agent framework |
| `x-handoff` | `{ from: 'agent-a', to: 'agent-b', reason: '...' }` | Orchestrator |
| `x-code-diff` | `{ file: '...', patch: '...' }` | Dev tools |

A generic client renders unknown types as raw JSON. A rich client with knowledge of custom types renders them with specialized UI.

### Turn Visibility

```typescript
type TurnVisibility =
  | { type: 'all' }
  | { type: 'participants'; ids: ParticipantId[] }
  | { type: 'role'; roles: ParticipantRole[] }
  | { type: 'private' };
```

